## Appendix C Angular beam divergence impact on collinearity and \(-t\) resolution

### Contribution of position resolution to collinearity width

Let us consider proton scattered at an angle \(\theta\), thus having momentum inclined at angles \(\theta_{x}\) and \(\theta_{y}\) with respect to \(z\)-axis. We can simply transform equations (2.3a) and (2.3b) to determine \(\theta\) angle components based on proton position measured in Roman Pot:

\[\theta_{x} =\frac{x}{L_{x}^{\rm eff}},\] (C.1a) \[\theta_{y} =\frac{y}{L_{y}^{\rm eff}}.\] (C.1b)

We can write uncertainty \(\delta\theta_{x/y}\) of the \(\theta_{x/y}\) due to finite position resolution \(\delta x/y\) as

\[\delta\theta_{x/y}=\frac{\delta x/y}{L_{x/y}^{\rm eff}},\] (C.2)

where

\[\delta x\approx\delta y\approx\frac{100\ \mu\rm m}{\sqrt{12}}\approx 29\ \mu\rm m,\quad L_{x}^{\rm eff}\approx L_{y}^{\rm eff}\approx 2 5\ \rm m.\]

Therefore

\[\delta\Delta\theta_{x/y}=\sqrt{2}\cdot\delta\theta_{x/y}\approx\sqrt{2}\cdot \frac{29\ \mu\rm m}{25\ \rm m}\approx 1.6\ \mu\rm rad.\] (C.3)

One can compare value obtained in (C.3) with the width of collinearity distribution presented on Figures 3.12 and 3.12, which varies from \(40\ \mu\rm rad\) to \(65\ \mu\rm rad\). Presented estimate of \(\delta x/y\) is rough, nonetheless the statement that position resolution has negligible contribution to collinearity stays intact. Assuming that scattering of \(100\ \rm GeV/c\) proton in the detector material can be neglected, main determinant of collinearity width lies in angular divergence of the beam.

### Uncertainty in \(-t\) reconstruction

To derive the error on \(-t\) due to angular divergence of interacting particles let us choose for convinience the situation, in which two protons are diverged in respect to \(z\)-axis only in \(yz\) plane (Figure 3.15). Considering a proton moving accordingly to \(z\)-axis direction (\(\alpha_{2}=\alpha\), \(\alpha_{2}^{*}=\alpha^{*}\)) we can write its four-momentum vector before (\(p_{1}\)) and after (\(p_{1}^{\prime}\)) elastic interaction:

\[p_{1}=(E,0,p\sin\alpha,p\cos\alpha),\] (C.4a)\[p_{1}^{\prime}=(E,0,p\sin{(\theta+\alpha^{*})},p\cos{(\theta+\alpha^{*})}).\] (C.4b)

Following the definition of Mandelstam \(t\) variable

\[t =(p_{1}^{\prime}-p_{1})^{2}=-p^{2}\left[\sin{(\theta+\alpha^{*})}- \sin{\alpha}\right]^{2}-p^{2}\left[\cos{(\theta+\alpha^{*})}-\cos{\alpha} \right]^{2}=\] \[=-p^{2}\left[\cos^{2}{(\theta+\alpha^{*})}-2\cos{(\theta+\alpha^{ *})}\cos{\alpha}+\cos^{2}{\alpha}+\sin^{2}{(\theta+\alpha^{*})}-2\sin{(\theta +\alpha^{*})}\sin{\alpha}+\sin^{2}{\alpha}\right]=\] \[=-2p^{2}\left[1-\cos{(\theta+\alpha^{*})}\cos{\alpha}-\sin{( \theta+\alpha^{*})}\sin{\alpha}\right].\] (C.5)

Using formulas for sine and cosine of the sum of two angles we obtain

\[t=-2p^{2}\left[1-\cos{\alpha}\left(\cos{\theta}\cos{\alpha^{*}}-\sin{\theta} \sin{\alpha^{*}}\right)-\sin{\alpha}\left(\sin{\theta}\cos{\alpha^{*}}+\cos{ \theta}\sin{\alpha^{*}}\right)\right].\] (C.6)

With the linear approximations

\[\cos{\alpha}\approx 1,\ \ \ \cos{\alpha^{*}}\approx 1,\ \ \ \sin{\alpha}\approx{\alpha},\ \ \ \sin{\alpha^{*}}\approx{\alpha^{*}},\]

we find simplified form of (C.6):

\[t=-2p^{2}\left[1-(1-\alpha\alpha^{*})\cos{\theta}-(\alpha+\alpha^{*})\sin{ \theta}\right].\] (C.7)

Assuming, that

\[\alpha\approx{\alpha^{*}}\]

we find

\[t=-2p^{2}\left[1-\cos{\theta}-2\alpha\sin{\theta}\right]+o\left(\alpha^{2} \right)=\underbrace{-2p^{2}\left(1-\cos{\theta}\right)}_{t_{\theta}}+4p^{2} \alpha\sin{\theta}+o\left(\alpha^{2}\right),\] (C.8)

where \(t_{\theta}\) refers to squared four-momentum transfered in case of parallel protons momenta (Eq. (1.12a)). Neglecting terms of the order \(\alpha^{2}\) and higher we find the smearing of \(-t\) due to beam divergence as

\[\delta t\equiv|t_{\theta}-t|=4p^{2}|\alpha|\sin{\theta}\approx 4p^{2}|\alpha|\theta.\] (C.9)

To see the measure of this effect let us use value \(40\ \mu\)rad as the mean value of \(|\alpha|\). It justified by average width of collinearity (affected mainly by beam divergence), which is \(55\ \mu\)rad, \(\sqrt{2}\) times more than single beam divergence. For \(-t\sim\widetilde{t}=0.01\ \mathrm{GeV}^{2}/\mathrm{c}^{2}\), value corresponding to detector acceptance, we get

\[\delta t_{-t\widetilde{t}}=4\cdot 100^{2}\cdot 40\cdot 10^{-6}\cdot 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{GeV}^{2}/ \mathrm{c}^{2}=0.0016\ \mathrm{GeV}^{2}/\mathrm{c}^{2}.\] (C.10)

Number above is in satisfactory agreement with single detector \(-t\) resolution determined with Monte Carlo simulation. To specify \(-t\) for elastic event first scattering angle components reconstructed in east and west detectors are averaged, therefore overall resolution on \(-t\) is much better, as shown on Figure 5.7.

